"The letters 'Ta-Sin-Mim' at the beginning of the surah convey a severe warning. These letters are the clear verses of the Quran. Rather than sending down a compelling sign from the sky, Allah wills to bring human beings to maturity through willing acceptance. The diverse, beautiful plants grown on the earth are evidence of Allah's oneness and power. Yet most people do not believe despite these signs. Your Lord is the Almighty, the Merciful; He does whatever He wills and shows mercy.\n\nMoses (Musa) was tasked with going to Pharaoh and his people. When Moses expressed his fears and stated that his tongue was tied, Allah also appointed Aaron (Harun) as a helper. Pharaoh asked, 'What is the Lord of the worlds?' Moses replied, 'He is the Lord of the heavens, the earth, and everything between them.' Pharaoh said to those around him, 'This messenger of yours is mad.' Moses answered by saying, 'He is the Lord of the east, the west, and all that is between them.' Pharaoh threatened that he would throw him into prison. Moses cast his staff, and it turned into a serpent, and drew out his hand, showing a radiant white light.\n\nPharaoh accused Moses of sorcery and gathered the magicians. The magicians threw their ropes and staffs, but Moses' staff swallowed them up. Thereupon, the magicians fell down in prostration and believed. Pharaoh threatened to crucify them. Afterwards, Allah revealed to Moses that he should set out by night with the Children of Israel. Pharaoh assembled soldiers and pursued them. When Moses struck the sea with his staff, the sea parted; the Children of Israel were saved, while Pharaoh and his soldiers were drowned. In this there is a lesson, but most of them did not believe.\n\nThe story of Abraham is related: he questioned his father and his people regarding their worship of idols. They said that they had found their fathers doing so. Abraham stated that he had taken the Lord of the worlds as his friend, that He created him, guides him to the right path, feeds him and gives him drink, heals him when he falls ill, will cause him to die and then bring him back to life, and from whom he hopes for forgiveness of his faults on the Day of Judgment. He prayed, 'Grant me wisdom, join me with the righteous, grant me an honorable mention among later generations, and make me an inheritor of the Garden of Delight (Jannat al-Na'im).' In this story too there is a sign.\n\nThe people of Noah considered their prophet a liar. Noah invited them to fear Allah, but they refused to believe, saying, 'Only the lowest people have followed you.' Noah told them that their reckoning rested with Allah alone. In the end, they were destroyed by the flood. The people of 'Ad rejected Hud as a liar. Hud criticized them for building ostentatious monuments on every hill and seizing people with tyranny. They were destroyed. The people of Thamud rejected Salih as a liar. Salih brought the she-camel as a miracle, yet they slaughtered the camel and suffered the punishment.\n\nThe people of Lot rejected their prophet as a liar. Lot forbade them from turning to men, but they threatened him with exile. Lot and his family were saved, while the rest were destroyed, except for an old woman. The people of Aykah rejected Shu'ayb as a liar. Shu'ayb commanded them to give full measure, not to depreciate people's goods, and not to cause corruption. Instead, they demanded that he cause a fragment of the sky to fall upon them. They were destroyed by the punishment of the Day of the Shadow. All these stories are lessons for the disbelievers.\n\nThe Quran has been sent down by the Lord of the worlds. The Trustworthy Spirit (Gabriel) brought it down. It was revealed in a clear Arabic tongue. It is mentioned in the earlier scriptures as well. The scholars of the Children of Israel know it. Had it been sent down to someone who did not know Arabic, they still would not have believed. It has been made to penetrate the hearts of the sinners, yet they will not believe until they behold the painful punishment. The punishment will strike them suddenly. At that moment they will plead for respite, whereas while in the world they sought to hasten it. Even if they were granted enjoyment for years, it will be of no avail to them once the punishment arrives.\n\nWarning prophets were certainly sent to every destroyed land. They were duly cautioned, and Allah did not wrong them in any way. The devils did not bring down the Quran. It is neither fitting for them nor within their capability, for they are kept far away from hearing revelation. They are forbidden from listening to the discourse in the Exalted Assembly (al-Mala' al-A'la).\n\nThis Quran has been sent down by the Lord of the worlds. The Trustworthy Spirit (Jibril) brought it down. The Trustworthy Spirit is the angel entrusted with Allah’s trust, who faithfully conveys the revelation to the prophets. The Quran was sent down into the Prophet's heart, placed in his memory, and ingrained as a faculty in him. As stated in the tafsir of Najm al-Din al-Kubra, had the Torah also been revealed directly to the heart in this manner, Moses would not have cast down the tablets nor gone to al-Khidr.\n\nThe Quran was sent down in a clear Arabic language. It was also mentioned in the earlier scriptures. The fact that the scholars of the Children of Israel knew it is evidence against the deniers. Had the Quran been sent down to someone who does not know Arabic, they still would not have believed. This stems from the stubbornness of the deniers. They understand it, yet because they are sinners they do not believe—until they see the punishment.\n\nBeware of supplicating and worshiping anyone other than Allah. Warn your nearest kinsfolk. When this verse was revealed, the Prophet climbed Mount Safa, summoned his relatives, and warned them. Be humble toward the believers. If they disobey you, say, \"I am free of what you do.\" Put your trust in Allah, the Almighty, the Merciful. He sees you when you rise for prayer at night and observes your movements among those who prostrate. He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.\n\nThe devils descend upon every sinful, slanderous liar. They lend their ears to the devils, and most of them are liars. The character of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), however, is the exact opposite of this; he is characterized by truthfulness and trustworthiness.\n\nAs for the poets, the deviants follow them. The poets wander aimlessly in every valley and say what they do not do. Excepted, however, are those who believe, perform righteous deeds, remember Allah often, and defend themselves when they are wronged. Believing poets such as 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah and Hassan ibn Thabit are outside this scope. And those who do wrong will soon know what kind of end they will meet."